      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "During the last decade many new biological immune modulators have entered the market as new therapeutic principles. Tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α is a pro-inflammatory cytokine known to a have a key role in the pathogenic mechanisms of various immune-mediated or inflammatory diseases. However, TNF-α also plays a key role in endothelial dysfunction and, thus, in the development and progression of atherosclerosis. What, then, is the potential therapeutic role of TNF-α inhibitors?"
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We analysed the current literature concerning the administration of TNF-α inhibitors and their potential benefits upon endothelial function."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "TNF-α inhibitors decrease the serum levels of inflammatory markers such as TNF-α itself, CRP, IL-6, and increased the tissue expression of endothelial NO synthase and the vasodilatory response to bradykinin."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "TNF-α inhibitors may change the progression of endothelial dysfunction and, thus, slow down the atherosclerotic process."
        }
      ]},
